WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.700
These are some unusual pictures of Kim&nbsp;
Jong Un, North Korea's supreme leader.&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.380
Unusual because they show him in a&nbsp;
context we don’t normally see him.&nbsp;

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:16.920
Let’s zoom back a bit... all these&nbsp;
people around him are scientists.
 &nbsp;

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:22.620
Thanks to their research, North Korea has&nbsp;
developed its weapons of mass destruction.&nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.980
But they didn't do this alone.
 &nbsp;

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.680
North Korean scientists have long tried&nbsp;
to partner with foreign universities.&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:38.280
And some of these collaborations may have&nbsp;
been trojan horses used by the regime to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:43.140
access sensitive information.
So, we took a closer look.
 &nbsp;

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:49.860
Our investigation focused on an alarming&nbsp;
number of joint publications with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:57.660
prestigious laser institute... in Germany!
This most likely violates U.N. sanctions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:03.540
ban scientific collaborations with North Korea.
 
So, how was this collaboration possible?&nbsp;

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.040
And did it potentially help North Korea build&nbsp;
more modern weapons of mass destruction?
&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:21.000
Our story starts here, at the&nbsp;
Max Born Institute in Berlin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.300
or MBI - a prestigious German institute&nbsp;
conducting basic research on lasers.
 &nbsp;

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:35.760
In 2008 and 2009, MBI took in&nbsp;
two North Korean physicists.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:42.660
Dr. Im Son Jin, who stayed for nearly two years.&nbsp;

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.780
And Dr. Kim Kwang Hyon, who&nbsp;
stayed for nearly three.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:52.680
Both came with a German scholarship, both worked&nbsp;
with MBI researcher Doctor Joachim Hermann.
 &nbsp;

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:57.720
The personal relationships, especially between&nbsp;
Hermann and Im, will play a crucial part in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.420
this story. But for now, all you need to know is&nbsp;
that the presence of two North Korean physicists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:15.960
at MBI should have probably raised a red flag.
This is Katsuhisa Furukawa. He was part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.900
UN Panel of Experts that monitors&nbsp;
sanctions against North Korea.&nbsp;

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:42.900
But the regime turned to its own scientists.
Instead of importing weapon-related technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.580
from abroad, Pyongyang focused on&nbsp;
developing its own. And it worked.
 &nbsp;

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:55.500
Since its first test in 2006, North Korea has&nbsp;
conducted 5 more tests with nuclear weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:01.020
and more than 200 with ballistic missiles –&nbsp;
especially this year saw an unprecedented number.
 &nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.420
Today, Kim Jong Un can boast that his&nbsp;
claimed hydrogen bomb was "homemade".&nbsp;

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.860
But the science behind these&nbsp;
weapons likely comes from abroad.&nbsp;

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:37.320
The James Martin Center for Nonproliferation&nbsp;
Studies in the U.S. developed an interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:44.340
dataset to look into this concern. Basically,&nbsp;
it counts how often North Koreans have published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:48.600
papers with foreign scientists.
 
This graph shows that collaborations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:53.220
skyrocketed after the first sanctions&nbsp;
of 2006 restricted technology transfer.&nbsp;

00:03:53.220 --> 00:04:01.140
In 2016 the UN Security Council finally&nbsp;
decided to close this loophole. New sanctions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.940
forbade almost every type of collaboration.
 
Unsurprisingly, the country mostly involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.860
is North Korea's big brother China.
But second place goes to... Germany!&nbsp;

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:15.300
A liberal democracy and one of&nbsp;
the UN’s biggest supporters.
 &nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:20.460
So how was this possible?
Enter science diplomacy.&nbsp;

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:39.180
Basically, it's one way to&nbsp;
build trust between nations.&nbsp;

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.660
For example, even as the cold war raged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:47.040
scientists in the US and Soviet Union&nbsp;
worked together on a polio vaccine.&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:55.560
Efforts like this helped build trust and&nbsp;
eventually led to arm control treaties.
 &nbsp;

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:00.600
Science is also crucial in solving&nbsp;
common problems, like climate change.&nbsp;

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.720
Or in the case of North Korea,&nbsp;
to monitor their active volcano.
 &nbsp;

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.340
But there's more.
Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.760
countries, including Germany,&nbsp;
have a constitutional tradition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.840
that guarantees freedom of science to&nbsp;
protect its independence from politics.&nbsp;

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:32.880
Kai Gehring is a German politician from&nbsp;
the Green Party who heads the Science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:06:08.100
Committee in parliament.
And in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:12.900
a red line may have been crossed…
Which brings us back to the MBI.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:18.180
Neither Im nor Kim are above suspicion.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:25.860
Im worked with scholars from a Chinese academy&nbsp;
that could be linked to Beijing's nuclear program.&nbsp;

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:32.760
Whereas Kim, in a statement, supported&nbsp;
North Korea's "building of nuclear force"
 &nbsp;

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:41.820
After coming back to North Korea, Dr. Im, in&nbsp;
particular, kept in touch with Dr. Herrmann.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:48.300
We reached him by email, and he told us that&nbsp;
they are still in contact. The last paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:52.620
they published together is dated 2020.
 
In total, nine papers were published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:59.340
after the 2016 sanctions were introduced.
 
And their subject is... well, very, very complex.&nbsp;

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:15.120
But what matters in our case is whether&nbsp;
the research has dual-use potential and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:21.000
can be used by the military.
We asked 10 physicists and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.200
non-proliferation experts separately&nbsp;
to analyze some of these papers.
 &nbsp;

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.980
Some physicists considered them&nbsp;
"irrelevant" to military applications.&nbsp;

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:33.240
Many pointed out that "everything in&nbsp;
physics has a possible dual application"&nbsp;

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:37.500
While others said they could be used to&nbsp;
develop weapons, even if it’s in the future.
 &nbsp;

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.980
However, all non-proliferation&nbsp;
experts were alarmed.
 &nbsp;

00:07:42.780 --> 00:08:14.460
A UN source, who requested anonymity, told us:
So why did MBI researcher Herrmann continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:19.440
to work with North Korean scientists?
Well, that's exactly what we asked the institute.&nbsp;

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:41.760
This is Marc Vrakking, MBI's director.
He told us that there is another important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:09:00.060
reason why the collaboration continued…
In other words, they didn't know.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:03.720
In a written statement the MBI pointed&nbsp;
out that the institute eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:07.440
stopped Herrmann’s work "because of North&nbsp;
Korea’s role in international politics."&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:19.560
and added:
So, are politicians to blame?&nbsp;

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:47.880
This case shows that autocratic&nbsp;
regimes can exploit scientific freedom:&nbsp;

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:52.980
- German politics failed to communicate&nbsp;
and properly enforce UN sanctions.&nbsp;

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:56.520
- The MBI never asked German&nbsp;
authorities for any permission.&nbsp;

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:01.980
- And the whole system could be&nbsp;
bypassed through personal relationships.&nbsp;

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:18.180
In order to avoid dangerous technologies falling&nbsp;
into the wrong hands, German lawmakers might need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:21.900
to draw up clearer red lines.
 
But neither politicians nor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:36.120
scientists can do this alone.
The BIG challenge will be to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:40.920
the transfer of sensitive information while at&nbsp;
the same time protecting scientific freedom.

